      "text": "We begin by identifying the major and minor axes of the ellipse. According to the diagram, the lengths of the major and minor axes are given as 2a = 6 and 2b = 2, respectively. To find the y-axis which is parallel to the coordinate axes and at a unit point, we set minor axis length under the equation:\n\n$$2b=2\\Rightarrow b=1$$\n\nUsing the given values for the major and minor axis lengths, we can find the length of the semi-major axis and semi-minor axis; this is given by:\n\n$$a=\\frac{6}{2}=3$$\n$$b=\\frac{2}{2}=1$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of the foci, we make use of the focal distance formula:\n\n$$c=\\sqrt{a^2-b^2}$$\n\nSubstituting the values of a and b, we get:\n\n$$c=\\sqrt{3^2-1^2}=\\sqrt{9-1}=\\sqrt{8}$$\n\nThus, the distance between the foci of the ellipse is $2c$ which is $2\\sqrt{8}=4\\sqrt{2}$. So, the final answer is:\n\n$$\\boxed{4\\sqrt{2}}$$",
